From: Petr Wozniak <petr.wozniak@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] map-controller 99993: do not let an unmeasured link erase a measured rcpi

An MLO client is reported once per affiliated link. A link the client is not
transmitting on has nothing to measure and honestly sends rcpi 0. All of those
reports land on the single row that MLD has in the sta table, and both writers
of de_sta->rcpi are unconditional, so whichever report arrives last decides.
With three links and one carrying traffic, the measured value loses two times
out of three.

Measured on hardware 2026-08-03, iPhone associated with three links on 4g:

    map.agent status                     mapc.db
    c6:f7:66:1a:0f:91  rssi -43          rcpi 0
    7a:c0:11:cb:0a:f3  rssi 0
    1a:37:b1:44:c0:70  rssi 0

The client sat at -43 dBm and the database said nothing was measured. Every
rcpi-based decision - steering thresholds, hysteresis, reporting - was taken on
that. The value had already travelled correctly from the driver through libwifi
(patch 940) and through map-agent; it was destroyed at the last step.

Note this is not an agent bug. Reporting 0 for an idle link is correct: that
BSS really did not measure anything. The defect is that the controller keeps one
row per MLD and treats an absent reading as a reading.

Both 0 and 255 mean no reading here - 255 is the not-available value from the
spec, 0 is what rssi_to_rcpi() produces for an unmeasured link - so neither is
allowed to overwrite a real one. A first report is still stored even when empty,
so a client that has genuinely never been measured does not appear to have been.

Cost: a client that leaves keeps its last rcpi until the row is released. That
is the right trade. Association state says whether the client is there; rcpi
says how well it was heard while it was.
--- a/src/cntlr_map.c
+++ b/src/cntlr_map.c
@@ -2513,7 +2513,31 @@
 		if (s) {
 			s->de_sta->dl_est_thput = dl_est_thput;
 			s->de_sta->ul_est_thput = ul_est_thput;
-			s->de_sta->rcpi = rcpi;
+			/* An MLO client is reported once per affiliated link, and a link
+			 * the client is not transmitting on has nothing to measure, so it
+			 * honestly sends rcpi 0. All those reports land on the one row this
+			 * MLD has and the last writer wins, so a measured value is routinely
+			 * erased by an unmeasured one.
+			 *
+			 * Measured on hardware 2026-08-03, iPhone with three links on 4g:
+			 *   link 2  rssi -43   -> rcpi 134
+			 *   link 1  rssi   0   -> rcpi 0
+			 *   link 0  rssi   0   -> rcpi 0
+			 * and mapc.db held rcpi 0 while the client sat at -43 dBm.
+			 *
+			 * Keep what was actually measured. Both 0 and 255 mean no reading:
+			 * 255 is the not-available value from the spec, 0 is what
+			 * rssi_to_rcpi() produces for an unmeasured link. Neither may
+			 * overwrite a real one.
+			 *
+			 * Cost: a client that leaves keeps its last rcpi until the row is
+			 * released. That is the right trade, because association state says
+			 * whether the client is there and rcpi says how well it was heard.
+			 */
+			if (rcpi && rcpi != 255)
+				s->de_sta->rcpi = rcpi;
+			else if (!s->de_sta->rcpi)
+				s->de_sta->rcpi = rcpi;
 
 			if (s->is_bsta) {
 				int est_thput = cntlr_estimate_max_throughput_for_node(c, n->almacaddr);
@@ -2938,7 +2962,12 @@
 			s->de_sta->time_delta = BUF_GET_BE32(b->time_delta);
 			/* record link-metrics-response receive time */
 			time(&s->de_sta->tsp);
-			s->de_sta->rcpi = b->ul_rcpi;
+			/* Same reason as above: do not let an unmeasured link erase a
+			 * measured one on the single row an MLD client has. */
+			if (b->ul_rcpi && b->ul_rcpi != 255)
+				s->de_sta->rcpi = b->ul_rcpi;
+			else if (!s->de_sta->rcpi)
+				s->de_sta->rcpi = b->ul_rcpi;
 
 			offset += sizeof(*b);
 		}
